Likutay Halachos

Yoreh De'ah · Laws of Interest (Ribbis) · Halacha 1

The matter of ribbis (interest on loans): according to popular thinking, it seems to run counter to plain reason — for it would appear fitting that the lender receive payment for the use of his money during the period it lay idle with him. Yet in truth the Torah prohibited ribbis, and the prohibition is exceedingly severe — as if one who takes it denies the fundamental principle of faith.

For it is found in the words of our Rebbe, may his light shine, that all the flow of abundance (shefa) of money that comes to a person derives from the aspect of nefesh (soul) — which is the aspect of nukva (the feminine, receptive principle), the aspect of raglayim (the feet/lower extremities). See the teaching "Gezailah" (LM Tinyana §69). Now when a person lends money to his fellow, through this he acquires him — as our Sages, of blessed memory, said: "the borrower is slave to the lender" (Mishlei 22:7) — for the borrower's very body becomes subordinate and obligated to him. And when the borrower himself has been obligated to the lender — that is, the obligating of his body — then from this automatically all his possessions become obligated to him as well. For when the borrower accepts money from the lender and becomes a slave and subordinate to him — as it is said, "the borrower is slave to the lender..." — through this he is included and becomes the aspect of nefesh to the lender, in the aspect of "whoever acquires a soul through his silver" — for when a person acquires a slave, he acquires his very soul, as it is said: "and the souls that they had made in Charan" (Bereishis 12:5) — and Rashi there explains: slaves and maidservants that they had acquired. The verse calls them by the name "souls," for the essence of acquisition is the nefesh, for once the person is his monetary acquisition, it follows that he is the aspect of his nefesh, for all possessions of a person are in the aspect of nefesh, as above — as is explained in the words of our Rebbe, may his light shine, there, see there.

And so too the borrower, who has been obligated to the lender through having received money from him, is in the aspect of his nefesh, as above. And through the obligating of the borrower himself, through this all the possessions of the borrower automatically become obligated to the lender — for all possessions are in the aspect of nefesh, as above. And since the lender has acquired the nefesh of the borrower for all the days of the duration of the loan, it follows that he takes from the borrower great reward on account of the waiting with the money — that is, the flow of abundance of money which the lender receives from the aspect of the nefesh of the borrower — for every nefesh has its flow of abundance of money, as is explained in the words of our Rebbe, may his memory be a blessing, there, see there. Therefore it is forbidden to take additional interest from him — for he has already received great reward all the days of the loan, namely the flow of abundance of money that he received from the nefesh of the borrower, as above. And therefore its prohibition is exceedingly severe, for in this prohibition the Sitra Achra gains its greatest hold — for the borrower is called ba'al chov (a debtor), which is the aspect of idolatry (avodah zarah), which is called chovah — as is found in the words of our Rebbe, may his light shine, in §23. And therefore a person must guard himself very greatly from being a ba'al chov (one who carries debt), and in any case the loan must be made in a permitted manner according to the Torah. But when a prohibition is created in the loan — that is, the prohibition of ribbis — then the Sitra Achra, which is the aspect of chovah, the aspect of idolatry, as above, gains a very strong foothold there — for it has a hold there, as above.

And therefore it is counted as if he denies — which is the aspect of idolatry — for in truth one must believe that all abundance flows only from the Blessed One. And when one believes that the abundance of money comes from above, certainly he will not take ribbis from the borrower — for he has already received a great deal from the borrower from the aspect of his nefesh, as above.

Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman

Translator's Notes

Source and structure: This translation renders Halacha 1 of Hilchos Ribbis (Laws of Interest) from Likutay Halachos, Yoreh De'ah, authored by Reb Nosn of Breslov, the primary disciple of Rebbe Nachman of Breslov. This is a compact halacha with no subdivisions — a single, continuous exposition following the opening ruling.

Central teaching: The foundational insight here derives from LM Tinyana §69 ("Gezailah"): all monetary abundance flows from the spiritual dimension of nefesh (soul). When a person lends money, the borrower becomes spiritually obligated to the lender — his very soul, as it were, becomes the lender's "acquisition." Through this, the lender already receives a hidden spiritual reward — the flow of the borrower's nefesh-abundance — all the days of the loan. To then demand ribbis (interest) on top of this is to take a double payment, and worse: it connects the debt to the Sitra Achra (the "other side"), which is called chovah (obligation/debt), i.e., idolatry — for idolatry "tips the scales toward culpability." Taking interest is thus tantamount to affirming that the avodah zarah yields fruit — which is the denial of the truth that all abundance comes from the Holy One alone.

Note on Halacha 4: As noted at the end of Avodas Elilim Halacha 3 in the source text, Hilchos Ribbis Halacha 4 is contained within Hilchos Avodas Elilim Halacha 3 — and has therefore already been translated there.

Ashkenazic transliteration follows throughout (e.g., ribbis, nefesh, nukva, raglayim, Sitra Achra, shefa, chovah, ba'al chov).

Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman
